    "q-64708": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64708",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $y=y(t)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-10y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(t)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-10{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-5$ or $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-5t}$ and ${e}^{2t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $-3{e}^{-5t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ is not a solution.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-64708-a",
